Wikitext Gotchas

1. `$reveal` cannot fragment HTML structure

`$reveal` renders as a widget node. You CANNOT use it to conditionally open/close HTML tags across boundaries — TW's widget tree doesn't support loose closing/opening tags inside widget bodies.

```
<!-- BROKEN — renders literal </div><div> text -->
<$reveal type="match" state="..." text="split">
<div class="pane">
</$reveal>

<!-- CORRECT — use CSS class toggle -->
<$let layoutClass={{{[[$:/state/layout]get[text]match[split]then[split-class]]}}}>
<div class=<<layoutClass>>>
  <div class="pane-a">...content...</div>
  <div class="pane-b">...content...</div>
</div>
</$let>
```

Key principle: Always keep HTML structure intact within a single widget scope. Use CSS classes to change layout modes.

---

2. Pragmas only work at the TOP of a tiddler — and an `\import`ed tiddler must not START with `\import`

`\procedure`, `\function`, `\widget`, `\import` only register before any content. Once a normal line (even a heading) appears, later pragmas are body text and the procedure silently doesn't exist.

When tiddler B does `\import [[A]]`, TW collects A's definitions only until the first non-definition. If A's first line is itself `\import`, NONE of A's procedures reach B. Put `\import` at the call site, never at the top of a shared-definitions tiddler.

`\* ... *\` (JS module header style) is not a comment either — it ends pragma processing at that line.

Note: `code-body: yes` only changes how the tiddler is DISPLAYED in the story river; it does not stop pragma parsing. To really disable parsing use `type: text/plain`.

---

3. `<%-- --%>` is NOT a valid comment

TW has NO wikitext comment syntax `<%-- ... --%>`. This renders as literal text. HTML comments `<!-- -->` are stripped. The `<%if%>` / `<%else%>` / `<%endif%>` conditional syntax exists, but there is no comment variant.

```
<!-- BROKEN — renders as visible text -->
<%-- this is not a comment --%>

<!-- CORRECT — use HTML comments -->
<!-- this is a comment -->
```

---

4. Use `$let` instead of nested `$set`

`$let` is more efficient (no tiddler context creation) and supports multiple variables:

```
<!-- Anti-pattern -->
<$set name="a" value="1"><$set name="b" value="2">...</$set></$set>

<!-- Better -->
<$let a="1" b="2">...</$let>
```

`$set` is still needed for: `filter` attribute (computed value), `select` attribute, `tiddler`/`subtiddler` context.

---

5. TW wraps inline wikitext in `<p>` tags

When wikitext content isn't in block mode, TW wraps it in `<p>`. This matters for test comparisons and CSS child selectors (`.a > .b` breaks when a `<p>` sits in between).

Procedures render in INLINE mode by default: `<<proc>>`, `<$macrocall>` and `<$transclude $variable="proc"/>` show headings (`!!`) and lists (`*`) as literal text. For block output use:

```
<$transclude $variable="proc" $mode="block"/>
```

---

6. `<%if%>`/`<%else%>` + `invokeActions` bug

Action widgets inside `<%else%>` branches are NOT reachable by `Widget.invokeActions()`. The else branch is stored in `elseChildren` which `invokeActions` does not traverse.

```
<!-- BROKEN — action in else branch never fires -->
<%if [<condition>]%>
  <$action-log message="true"/>
<%else%>
  <$action-log message="false"/>
<%endif%>

<!-- CORRECT — use $list with emptyMessage -->
<$list filter="[<condition>]" variable="ignore">
  <$action-log message="true"/>
</$list>
<$list filter="[<condition>]" emptyMessage="""<$action-log message="false"/>"""/>
```

Read-only rendering (not actions) works fine with `<%if%>`.

Related: never leave the THEN branch empty. `<%if [<x>match[yes]]%><%else%>BODY<%endif%>` renders the matched value (`yes`) as text when the condition is true. Invert the condition instead: `<%if [<x>match[no]]%>BODY<%endif%>`.

---

7. Nested `{{{ }}}` is invalid — filter expressions cannot nest

`{{{...}}}` (filtered transclusion) cannot contain another `{{{...}}}`. The inner triple braces are not evaluated — they become literal text, producing broken output.

```
<!-- BROKEN — inner {{{...}}} is literal text -->
<$button class={{{ [[prefix-]addsuffix{{{ [<x>match[y]then[active]] }}}] }}}>

<!-- CORRECT — use $let to pre-compute the inner value -->
<$let suffix={{{[<x>match[y]then[active]]}}} cls={{{[[prefix-]addsuffix<suffix>]}}}>
<$button class=<<cls>>>
</$let>

<!-- CORRECT — combine into one filter expression -->
<$button class={{{ [<x>match[y]then[prefix-active]else[prefix-]] }}}>
```

Rule: If you need a computed value inside a filter, break it into a `$let` chain. Each `{{{...}}}` must be a standalone, non-nested expression.

---

8. `{{{ }}}` only works in widget attributes — not in `<<procedure>>` calls

Filtered transclusion `{{{...}}}` is valid inside widget attributes like `<$widget attr={{{ filter }}}/>`. It does NOT work as an argument to a `<<procedure>>` macro-style call.

```
<!-- BROKEN — {{{ }}} is not evaluated in procedure calls -->
<<my-procedure arg:{{{ [<x>get[field]] }}}>>

<!-- CORRECT — use $transclude to call the procedure with computed args -->
<$transclude $variable="my-procedure" arg={{{ [<x>get[field]] }}}/>

<!-- CORRECT — or let the procedure read the variable from the caller's scope -->
<$let val={{{ [<x>get[field]] }}}>
<<my-procedure>>   <!-- procedure body uses <<val>> directly -->
</$let>
```

Also: positional arguments are LITERAL text. `<<show myvar>>` passes the string `myvar`, not its value. And `<<show <<myvar>>>>` breaks the parser and leaks closing tags as text — never nest `<< >>`.

Rule: `<<procedure>>` calls only accept literal values. For computed arguments, always use `<$transclude $variable="name" arg={{{ filter }}}/>` instead.

---

9. `\define` substitution syntax does NOT work in `\procedure` or `\function`

`\define` performs textual substitution BEFORE parsing: `$param$` inserts a parameter value, `$(varname)$` inserts an outer variable. `\procedure` and `\function` do NOT support either form — they become literal text.

Never use `\define`. Always use `\procedure`, `\function`, or `\widget`. When migrating:

```
<!-- OLD — \define with $param$ and $(var)$ substitution -->
\define my-state() $:/state/view/$(currentTiddler)$/mode
\define my-filter(type) [rrt.type[$type$]sort[title]]

<!-- NEW — \function with filter operators -->
\function my-state() [<currentTiddler>addprefix[$:/state/view/]addsuffix[/mode]]
\function my-filter(type) [rrt.type<__type__>sort[title]]
```

Migration rules by context:
- **Computed paths** (`$:/state/foo/$(var)$/bar`): use `\function` with `addprefix`/`addsuffix`
- **Parameter access** (`$param$`): use `<<param>>` in procedure bodies, `<__param__>` in filter expressions
- **Nested macro refs** (`$(otherMacro)$`): use `\function` with `[function[otherMacro]]` chaining
- **Filter string construction** (building `[title[$(var)$]]` for subfilter): inline backtick template at the call site — backtick templates (`` `...$(var)$...` ``) support `$(var)$` regardless of pragma type

Exception: Inside backtick template strings (`` `...` ``), `$(var)$` substitution works with any pragma type. This is a separate mechanism from `\define`.

---

10. Widget attributes cannot concatenate

An attribute is ONE of `"literal"`, `<<variable>>`, `{{transclusion}}`, `{{{ filter }}}` or a backtick template. `tiddler=<<base>>/suffix` keeps only `<<base>>`.

```
<!-- BROKEN -->
<$link to=<<base>>/child/>

<!-- CORRECT -->
<$link to={{{ [<base>addsuffix[/child]] }}}/>
<$link to=`$(base)$/child`/>
```

A `{{{ filter }}}` attribute keeps only the FIRST result. Chain `+[format:titlelist[]join[ ]]` to pass a whole list.

Textref braces need a literal title: `{{<<var>>}}` does not work. Use `{{{ [<var>get[text]] }}}` (or `get[field]`).

---

11. `<<var>>` output is re-parsed as wikitext

A value like `$:/config/x` printed with `<<var>>` becomes a link (system-link syntax). Print raw values with `<$text text=<<var>>/>`. Attribute values (`tiddler=<<var>>`) are not re-parsed.

---

12. Widgets and HTML tags must nest cleanly

A `<$let>` opened inside a `<div>` must close inside it. Crossing boundaries (`<div><$let>…</div></$let>`) prints `</div>` as literal text. Wrap the whole HTML structure in the widget instead.

---

13. `$transclude $tiddler=` sets `thisTiddler`, NOT `currentTiddler` — and extra attributes are not variables

Inside `<$transclude $tiddler="X"/>`, `<currentTiddler>` is still the CALLER's tiddler; `<thisTiddler>` is X. Use `<$tiddler tiddler="X">` when you need currentTiddler to change.

Extra attributes like `<$transclude $tiddler="X" mode-flag="yes"/>` are NOT readable as `<<mode-flag>>` in X unless X declares `\parameters (mode-flag)`. Pass context with an outer `<$let>` instead.

---

14. Stylesheet tiddlers: `--` turns into an en-dash

Wikitext stylesheets (`$:/tags/Stylesheet`, default type) run typography rules, so `var(--accent)` becomes `var(–accent)` and silently breaks. Put `\rules except dash` at the top of the stylesheet body.

